PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
SHELL=/bin/bash
HOME=/var/trugeo
MAILTO=root
CRON_TZ=America/New_York
PGDATABASE=postgres
LOG_DIR=/var/log/trugeo

# NOTIFICATION VOLUME
#
# As shipped, every run mails on start and on success, which is about 50
# messages a day, dominated by the 24 hourly probe batches. That is deliberate
# while the free ip-api tier caps throughput at 45 calls a minute and a full
# probing cycle therefore takes weeks: each hourly batch is the only visible
# evidence of progress.
#
# ONCE COMMERCIAL ip-api ACCESS IS IN PLACE, a full cycle completes in a day or
# less, so per-batch mail stops being informative. Uncomment the two lines below
# to reduce probe mail to cycle boundaries and failures only.
#
# NOTIFY_START=0
# NOTIFY_SUCCESS=0
#
# ADDRESS FAMILY SCOPE
#
# IPv6 is out of scope by decision, so the default is IPv4 only. Uncomment the
# line below to bring IPv6 into scope when adoption makes it worthwhile.
#
# PROBE_IPV4_ONLY=0
#
# That ONE variable drives both the probe table populate in daily_pipeline.sh and
# the probe in probe_batch.sh. Do not set the family in one place only: if the
# populate loads a family that the probe never selects, those rows keep ran_at
# NULL forever and the cycle-complete gate never opens again, so the probe table
# is never rebuilt.
#
# Nothing else needs changing to support IPv6. geo_exclusions containment is
# family-correct, so an IPv4 rule cannot suppress an IPv6 range, and
# sampleRanges, apply_splits and classify_ranges all handle both families. What
# is missing is only exclusion DATA: no IPv6 government prefixes have been
# probe-verified, so none are seeded.
#
# PROBE TABLE REBUILD
#
# Off by default, because it changes what feeds the DMA export. Uncomment to
# enable once the history primary key has been changed to (network, ran_at) and a
# manual rebuild has been verified on this database.
#
# PROBE_TBL_REBUILD=1
#
# What each does:
#   NOTIFY_START=0     no "starting" mail
#   NOTIFY_SUCCESS=0   no "ran correctly, finishing" mail
#
# What you still receive with both set to 0:
#   every error, from any job, always. Error mail is not suppressible.
#   the "cycle complete" mail from probe_batch when the backlog reaches zero,
#     which is the end-of-cycle signal, sent regardless of these two settings.
#   lock-wait and slow-step alerts.
#
# These are cron-wide. To silence probe mail but keep the daily and monthly jobs
# verbose, delete the two lines above and append the variables to the probe job
# line instead, so that only it is affected:
#
# 5 * * * *   cronuser NOTIFY_START=0 NOTIFY_SUCCESS=0 /var/trugeo/pipeline/probe_batch.sh >/dev/null

# Each job discards stdout but NOT stderr.
#
# log() tees every line to both the log file and stdout, so without the
# redirect cron sees output on every single run and mails MAILTO regardless of
# exit code. That produced a duplicate of every notification: for probe_batch,
# three mails an hour instead of two, roughly 72 a day.
#
# Discarding stdout costs nothing, because tee still writes the log file, and
# the scripts send their own start, success and error mail. stderr is kept
# deliberately: the only thing that writes to it is the guard that fires when
# LOG_DIR is unwritable, which happens before the mail path is usable, so cron
# mail is the sole notification channel for exactly that failure.

0 2 * * *   cronuser /var/trugeo/pipeline/daily_pipeline.sh >/dev/null
5 * * * *   cronuser /var/trugeo/pipeline/probe_batch.sh >/dev/null
0 0 2 * *   cronuser /var/trugeo/pipeline/monthly_dbip.sh >/dev/null
